## [claude] 2026-10-11 13:40 | P1.claude | research findings: brief structure, RPG fundamentals, asset canvas, LTS, tooling
DONE (PARALLEL P1, my half):

A. Example brief (structure only; its content is another project and is ignored). What a brief that an agent can execute alone needs:
 1. A one-sentence mission + a single "gold path": the exact sequence a viewer watches, each step with a pass condition.
 2. Priorities in tiers (P0/P1) AND an explicit "not needed unless every P0 passes" list. Scope is defined by what is cut.
 3. Art direction as a short style statement + a style bible file + concept art approved BEFORE any 3D.
 4. Per-system sections (characters/pipeline, enemies/NPCs, world, gameplay incl. controls/camera/combat/progression, VFX+sound as required not optional, front-end, HUD, audio, architecture).
 5. Generation tools + budget table (need -> first choice -> fallback), max attempts per asset, log every paid call.
 6. Milestones, each with a gate that is checkable. "Always playable" from the first greybox milestone; revert breaking changes after 20 min.
 7. Verification by something other than the author: an in-game driver that plays the gold path and writes numbers + screenshots, and a fresh-context reviewer scoring captures against the approved concepts. Numbers and screenshots each catch what the other misses.
 8. Progress documentation as a deliverable (fixed story cameras, timelapse captures).
 9. Engine practice lessons (compile/refresh discipline, Input System only, uGUI for HUD so captures see it).
 => Proposal: our GDD uses this skeleton, so the GDD is directly the master build prompt the boss asked for.

B. RPG design fundamentals (saved page; examples in it name other games, I keep only the principles):
 - Three pillars: player actions (rules/verbs), progression (getting stronger), narrative (context that gives the verbs meaning). Decide the sub-genre first; it fixes the expected mechanics. Ours is real-time action RPG: third-person camera, real-time combat, spells/abilities, defensive actions, item use, skill/ability tree, equipment upgrades; traversal: fast travel, hidden areas, NPC interaction.
 - "Rules first, then weld the world to the rules": a magic system implies lore about magic; the setting follows the mechanics, not the reverse.
 - Levels: objectives, landmarks (natural + built) for orientation, distinct regions with organic borders, layout loops, guidance by light/terrain/props/subtle environmental marks, enemy placement with a reason, environmental storytelling without dialogue, shortcuts that unlock on return, safe recovery points that are not inside fights.
 - Characters: archetype -> role -> look follows role and backstory -> skills (names, effects, growth) -> equipment -> levelling curve paced to the story -> specialisation branches -> factions last (faction sets reputation, gear, contacts).
 - Systems: modular ruleset; interactions that allow multiple approaches; tune difficulty only after testing; balance exploits against agency; inventory + encumbrance forces keep/sell choices; item value ties to local economies; outcome variability.
 - UI: HUD readable at a glance (complex menus are tolerated, a complex HUD is not); one visual language across menu, HUD, minimap; combat UI shows cooldowns cleanly; inventory with categories; quest log organised; contextual tooltips near objects.
 - Advanced: pick the primary audience early (single-player progression / self-expression / collection / narrative); mechanics must reinforce the theme; environment as a combat participant (surfaces, elements); progression must be VISIBLE on the character.

C. Asset canvas (what we own now, Unity project, all Humanoid-compatible animation):
 - Animation: ~540 clips. Unarmed root-motion locomotion 8-way + strafe, jump/fall/land, dive-roll, stun (Explosive RPG pack); ~42 fighting-motion strikes with height-specific hit/block reactions, knockdown/get-up (FightingMotions); 24 paired grab/throw clips (Brawler); ~90 spellcasting clips: one-hand/two-hand direct casts, charged load-then-cast, omni burst, casting stance in/out (Kevin Iglesias); 12 weapon styles with idle/walk/run/attack (Warrior bundles) + an 11-attack two-hand sword set; ~146 town-life clips: idles, talk, emotions, vending, carry, chant (MoCapCentral, Idle MoCap, Explosive crafting).
 - Environment: 90 village prefabs (houses, stalls, fences, gates, well, cart, banners, terrain tiles, rocks, trees), 35 modular tavern pieces + furniture, 14 painterly trees/bushes/grass.
 - VFX: 76 Hovl prefabs (lightning aura, electric hits, explosions, area effects, shields, portals, sparks, dust) + 241 Magic Arsenal prefabs (per element: charge, missile tiny-mega, muzzle, explosion, dome, beam). Magic Arsenal currently renders magenta (missing shaders) and 36 materials are still on the built-in Standard shader: both need a URP conversion step.
 - Characters: blocky demo mannequins + smoother mocap rigs. No final hero model yet: this is the biggest art gap and the natural first concept-art job (Higgsfield/Blender -> retarget to Humanoid).
 - Yoink-and-twist read: animation is our richest asset (fights, casts, town life) -> the design should lean on grounded close-quarters action + spellcasting + a living settlement. Environment set is a village/tavern scale, not a continent: a GDD that needs vast biomes would exceed the canvas.

